The per-stage floor is a code-generation artefact, not a hardware limit. The new candidate passes 16/16 workloads and is 16–18% faster than r3 at 373/384/449 on the rented B200.

**What causes the floor.** In our rings the MMA issuer is a single lane (`warp == 1 && lane == 0`). nvcc then wraps every `tcgen05.mma` in an ELECT/BRA.U.ANY loop (15–16 ELECTs in the kernel). Running the issuer loop on the whole warp and electing one lane only for the issue, as CUTLASS does, removes that loop. The ring then runs at the MMA ideal: 770 clk per stage at N=384, where 768 is ideal. The success targets were met at N=384 and with real W from DRAM, and narrowly missed at N=192 (686 against ≤650).

**Final rented test (16/16 correct, one launch), against r3-e2 on the same GPU:**

| B | this (µs) | r3-e2 (µs) | change |
|---|---|---|---|
| 373 | 57.8 | 68.6 | −15.8% |
| 384 | 56.3 | 68.3 | −17.6% |
| 449 | 67.8 | 80.5 | −15.7% |
| 512 | 73.9 | 80.7 | −8.5% |
| 691 | 102.2 | 110.6 | −7.7% |
| 773 | 114.0 | 130.6 | −12.7% |
| 853 | 122.3 | 132.3 | −7.6% |
| 919 | 130.1 | 133.3 | −2.4% |

- **By band:** S −0.5%, M −6.9%, L −9.2%. The emulator predicts a portal score of 0.640 ± 0.012, against 0.6166 for the current best; it was given no `paths`, so treat that as rough.
- **Dispatch:** B ≤ 256 still uses the r2/r3 kernels. In cycles, the new kernel was slower than r3's pair kernel there (612 against 514–546 clk per k-block at B=192 with the clock capped), and I did not find the cause.
- **Precision:** max error is 1.46–1.70e-3 on the first draws. One B=919 draw reached 1.72e-3, above the 1.7e-3 bar and below atol 2e-3. That is common for TF32 paths here, but it should be checked on 3 draws.
- **CUTLASS removed:** the B > 256 sizes no longer use CUTLASS, so its source files are gone and the build is faster.

### Rationale
The task was to bisect the 950–1200 clk per-stage floor (E2/H25). A resident-operand ablation in the probe, plus the SASS, shows the cause: a single-lane MMA issuer makes nvcc wrap each UTCHMMA in a uniformity loop. A CUTLASS-style ring removes it: a whole-warp TMA producer, a whole-warp MMA warp with an elected issue, and a multicast commit to the empty barriers. The ring then runs at 770 clk per stage at N=384, 768 being ideal; with real W from DRAM it is 830 clk at Bpad 384. The candidate dispatches B > 256 to a new two-accumulator warp-specialised 2SM kernel (`tc_ws`): one batch tile up to B=512 and two above. B ≤ 256 keeps the r2/r3 kernels, which were faster in cycles there. The portal gain should exceed the rented one: the hand kernels are cycle-bound at about 1.6 GHz, and CUTLASS ran at 1.96 GHz on the rented card.

